INT. MUNICIPAL SWAN-BOAT BOATHOUSE - EVENING

A cathedral of damp wood. Eleven fiberglass swans, enormous and faintly smug, are moored nose to tail in the dark water, each wearing the faded expression of a creature that has been pedaled by thousands of children and has forgiven none of them.

BARNABY QUILL (50s, municipal windbreaker, a lanyard heavy with keys that open nothing important) stands on the dock with a clipboard held like a hymnal. He is counting swans for the third time and arriving at a different number each time.

ODALYS FERREIRA-NNAMDI (40s, wet-weather poncho, hair in a hopeful bun) climbs out of a swan with the dignity of a woman leaving a courtroom.

ODALYS
It's twelve.

BARNABY
It's eleven.

ODALYS
The inventory says twelve.

BARNABY
The inventory also says the pond is a lake.

Behind them, a third voice drifts from the rafters. PETRU "PETE" LAZAR (20s, cheerful, harness, a flashlight in his teeth) dangles above the water, retrieving a pennant.

PETE
(around the flashlight)
Thith ith the one.

ODALYS
Pete, take the flashlight out of your mouth.

Pete spits the flashlight into his palm. It bounces off a swan's head and sinks.

BARNABY
That was a municipal flashlight.

PETE
It was my flashlight.

ODALYS
It was Barnaby's flashlight.

BARNABY
It was a flashlight in the general sense.

PETE
Can I come down now?

ODALYS
No.

BARNABY
Yes.

ODALYS
No, because if he comes down we'll have to talk about the swan.

BARNABY
We are talking about the swan.

PETE
Which swan?

ODALYS
The twelfth.

PETE
There's a twelfth?

BARNABY
There is not a twelfth.

ODALYS
Barnaby, I rode it. For twenty minutes. It has a steering problem and a name.

BARNABY
What name?

ODALYS
Gerald.

PETE
Gerald's a good name.

BARNABY
Pete, you are suspended in the air by one strap and I need you to stop having opinions.

PETE
I've had opinions the whole time, though.

ODALYS
He's had opinions since March.

BARNABY
In March I told you both the swans were not to be named.

ODALYS
You named the swans.

BARNABY
I named ONE swan. Lucille. Out of respect.

PETE
Lucille's on the left.

ODALYS
Lucille is the one we sank in August.

BARNABY
Lucille is resting.

A long silence. Water slaps the pilings. A swan, somewhere, knocks against the dock with a hollow note.

ODALYS
Barnaby, there are twelve swans.

BARNABY
(very quietly)
I know.

PETE
So who's got Gerald?

Barnaby lets the clipboard drop. It floats. All three of them watch it drift away across the dark water toward the far swan, which has, impossibly, begun to turn its head.
